From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Fri, 15 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tests: build test_audio as gnu99 so strdup() is declared

test_audio is the one target here built with -std=c99. Strict ANSI mode defines
__STRICT_ANSI__, under which glibc's string.h hides strdup() - it is POSIX,
not ISO C - so the strdup() call in Android's system/audio.h has no
declaration:

    system/audio.h: In function 'audio_device_address_to_parameter':
    system/audio.h:1002:12: error: implicit declaration of function 'strdup'

That was a warning until GCC 15, which defaults to -std=gnu23 and treats
implicit declarations as errors. It was never harmless: without a declaration
strdup() is assumed to return int, so the returned pointer was truncated to 32
bits on a 64-bit target.

test_audio pulls in Android hardware headers that use POSIX interfaces
throughout, so ask for the GNU dialect of the same standard.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
---
 tests/Makefile.am | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/tests/Makefile.am
+++ b/tests/Makefile.am
@@ -47,7 +47,7 @@
 endif
 
 test_audio_SOURCES = test_audio.c
-test_audio_CFLAGS = -std=c99\
+test_audio_CFLAGS = -std=gnu99\
 	-I$(top_srcdir)/include \
 	-I$(top_srcdir)/include/android
 test_audio_LDADD = \
